{"tool_name":"acidbio","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"acidbio","home":"https://github.com/hoffmangroup/acidbio","license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"Acidbio a system for automated testing and certification of bioinformatics file format interoperability.","addition_date":"2022-08-26T08:13:53.384166Z","last_update_date":"2022-08-26T08:13:53.387796Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool","Suite"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:acidbio","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"michael.hoffman@utoronto.ca","name":"Michael M Hoffman","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-4517-1562","typeEntity":"Person"},{"name":"Danielle Denisko"},{"name":"Eric G Roberts"},{"name":"Yi Nian Niu"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"public"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Deposition","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3431"},{"term":"Format validation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0336"},{"term":"Formatting","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_0335"},{"term":"Parsing","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_1812"},{"term":"Scaffolding","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3216"}]}],"name":"Acidbio","owner":"Chan019","publication":[{"doi":"10.1093/BIOINFORMATICS/BTAC327","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2022 The Author(s) 2022. Published by Oxford University Press.Motivation: Bioinformatics software tools operate largely through the use of specialized genomics file formats. Often these formats lack formal specification, making it difficult or impossible for the creators of these tools to robustly test them for correct handling of input and output. This causes problems in interoperability between different tools that, at best, wastes time and frustrates users. At worst, interoperability issues could lead to undetected errors in scientific results. Results: We developed a new verification system, Acidbio, which tests for correct behavior in bioinformatics software packages. We crafted tests to unify correct behavior when tools encounter various edge cases - potentially unexpected inputs that exemplify the limits of the format. To analyze the performance of existing software, we tested the input validation of 80 Bioconda packages that parsed the Browser Extensible Data (BED) format. We also used a fuzzing approach to automatically perform additional testing. Of 80 software packages examined, 75 achieved less than 70% correctness on our test suite. We categorized multiple root causes for the poor performance of different types of software. Fuzzing detected other errors that the manually designed test suite could not. We also created a badge system that developers can use to indicate more precisely which BED variants their software accepts and to advertise the software's performance on the test suite.","authors":[{"name":"Denisko D."},{"name":"Hoffman M.M."},{"name":"Niu Y.N."},{"name":"Roberts E.G."}],"date":"2022-07-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Bioinformatics","title":"Assessing and assuring interoperability of a genomics file format"},"pmcid":"PMC9237710","pmid":"35575355"}],"topic":[{"term":"Workflows","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_0769"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"Acidbio","home":"https://bio.tools/acidbio","license":"GPL-2.0","summary":"Acidbio a system for automated testing and certification of bioinformatics file format interoperability.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:acidbio/cmd/github.com","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":false,"sourcecode":{"free":true,"interpreted":true,"license":false,"public":true,"readme":true,"repository":{"anonymous":true,"online":true}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":4,"